-- title:
--   Tuple Interpolated Vertical Line Ne Gives Exists Some Eval Mismatch
-- statement:
--   **A different interpolated vertical line exposes a slice evaluation mismatch.**
--
--   Let `params` have a field model, let $u\in\mathbb{F}_q^m$, let $x_1,\ldots,x_k$ be distinct heights, and let $g_1,\ldots,g_k$ be completed outcomes. If at least $d+1$ coordinates are genuine and the tuple is globally consistent with one interpolated polynomial, then for every degree-$d$ line polynomial $f$ different from the resulting line polynomial $h$, some genuine coordinate disagrees:
--
--   $$
--   \exists i\in\{1,\ldots,k\},\qquad g_i\ne\bot\quad\land\quad g_i(u)\ne f(x_i).
--   $$
--
--   The proof uses that two degree-$d$ univariate polynomials agreeing at $d+1$ distinct heights are equal.
--
--   Formalization credit: Sirui Lu, Ruixuan Deng, Yanqiao Zhu, and Zhengfeng Ji (arXiv:2609.19814). Mathematical source: Zhengfeng Ji, Anand Natarajan, Thomas Vidick, John Wright, and Henry Yuen (arXiv:2009.12982).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The source declaration is `MIPStarRE.LDT.Pasting.tupleInterpolatedVerticalLine_ne_gives_exists_some_eval_mismatch`; its Lean statement gives the exact quantifier order and all boundary hypotheses.
